WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.890
JOANNA SMITH: With almost
500 sessions at Next 2019,

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:05.140
it's understandable that you
couldn't get to them all.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.780
So if you didn't
see API management

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.520
for serverless and
multi-cloud, then

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.680
stay tuned, because
here's the rewind.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.096
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.430
This talk is brought to
you by Prithpal Bhogill,

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.750
the PM for Apigee, and Kelsey
Hightower, a DevRel demo

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:21.583
favorite.

00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:23.000
It starts with
Prithpal explaining

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.910
some of the key advantages
of serverless, the fact

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:27.440
that it completely
abstracts away

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.000
the underlying infrastructure.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.640
It enables you to be
agile, because not worrying

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.160
about that infrastructure
allows you to focus and iterate

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:35.320
on your product.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.000
Serverless is perfect
for microservices,

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.890
which are easier to maintain.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:42.260
And the pair are quickly
becoming the go-to solution

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.440
for new systems--

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.260
or monolith migrations.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:47.660
And serverless has evolved.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.910
It started with
Functions as a Service

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:52.520
and then resonated so
well that it expanded

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.900
into building full applications,
and now, serverless containers.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.430
Oh, and fun fact--

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.680
a service is basically an API.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.030
No, stay with me here.

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:04.519
An API is a contract
around usage.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:06.800
Many service front-ends
are actually APIs.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.100
You build endpoints, and then
someone else will access them.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.330
APIs are the simplest mechanism
for connected data experiences

00:01:14.330 --> 00:01:16.400
between businesses,
within a business,

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.423
and from a business to a user.

00:01:18.423 --> 00:01:21.440
OK, so if the future
is microservices,

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.900
and that's basically
a series of APIs,

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:27.170
then we need to meet our newest
friend, the API management

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:28.430
platform.

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:31.490
With any API, it's important
to be able to simply accomplish

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.650
several operational tasks--

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:37.460
control and manage access to
the API, monitor usage quotas,

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:41.120
scale according to demand,
analyze usage metrics,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.380
and track monetization.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.850
So you see why API management
becomes a very critical

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.850
function as you start to roll
out more and more serverless

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:51.330
applications.

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:55.220
And this is where Prithpal
introduces Apigee Edge.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:57.110
The analytics system
in Apigee Edge

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:01.070
allows you to measure every
aspect of your API traffic.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.810
You can measure
operational details

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.583
like response time and frequency
of different types of errors.

00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:08.000
You can find things that are
interesting to your business,

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.270
like where are people located
when they call your APIs?

00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:14.330
Or which applications are
sending the most traffic?

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:17.540
OK, so now, in the talk,
Kelsey takes the stage

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:20.270
to show off what a
product like this can do.

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:23.240
He spends nearly 20 minutes
walking through a full example

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.170
for API management, which
includes Cloud Run, a simple Go

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:31.340
app, Curl, Docker files, Cloud
Build Apigee, simple IM auth

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.320
and XML configs, Cloud
Functions, and API proxy.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.440
And please don't ask me
to list all these again.

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.560
My point is that demos
like these, end to end,

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:42.410
showcasing the variety of
frameworks and applications

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.950
you may be using together
is why we do Cloud Next.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:47.550
So if you'd like to
see the full talk,

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.320
check out the link in
the description below.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.403
And don't forget to subscribe
to the Cloud YouTube

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:53.697
channel for more great
Next Rewind content.

00:02:53.697 --> 00:02:54.530
Thanks for watching.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.880
[MUSIC PLAYING]

